# =====================================================================
#  PART TWO: English text of the strings inside the processes
#  (parte2_proc.paw). The key is the SHA-1 of the Spanish string.
# =====================================================================

/STR 2cac5cbd
You talk to yourself. As governor, you can afford to.
/STR d3e76050
It's you: big belly, short body, long shanks. And now, governor.
/STR 3032d12c
Four silver reals. It isn't much, but it's everything.
/STR 723f4aba
A rope of a hundred fathoms, a present from Basilio. As long as a sermon.
/STR 1af57d4e
A gold jewel with a stone that shines like a piece of the sun. Worthy of Dulcinea.
/STR 468471be
A holland handkerchief, fine and large. It would do to blindfold someone.
/STR 3992281a
A thick cane, heavier than it looks.
/STR a4cad148
The helmet of Mambrino. It is of gold (or of brass), half the headpiece is missing and it has a notch for the beard. Sancho says it is a barber's basin.
/STR 53036f80
You blindfold yourself with the handkerchief. You can't see a thing, which is the whole point.
/STR 74109888
You are already blindfolded.
/STR 56490374
With what? You would need a handkerchief.
/STR dc0ee5d0
Under the mattress, where you hid them from the housekeeper, you find four reals. For a rainy day.
/STR 3920a950
Your bed. You know it well: a month in it.
/STR 2e1ada63
Beside the bed, hanging from a nail, are your arms. The housekeeper hasn't dared throw them away: she is afraid of them.
/STR 5dedf48b
You've slept quite enough: a whole month.
/STR 11bee953
Whom? Here are the bachelor SAMSON and SANCHO.
/STR 388153a8
A bachelor of Salamanca, about twenty-four, round-faced, flat-nosed and wide-mouthed: all signs of a mischievous disposition and a fondness for jokes and pranks.
/STR 050eb292
You are not leaving without a squire. Sancho is in the hall, with something to ask you.
/STR 5e69cdc3
Put on the corselet. A knight doesn't ride out in his doublet, not even at night.
/STR 737a89f0
Put on the helmet of Mambrino. A knight doesn't ride out bareheaded.
/STR 9f643e52
And the lance? It's in the hall, on its rack.
/STR 63a734e5
You are missing the buckler.
/STR 4105f0ce
You are missing the sword.
/STR a2e2dfa5
On foot? Mount Rocinante, who has put on weight and is raring to go.
/STR 3dc25d77
There is no turning back. At least not until you are defeated.
/STR d61ca86b
The farmer has already gone. You can hear his ballad in the distance.
/STR c16b77f8
A farm lad with two mules and a plough. He sings very badly and very loudly.
/STR b0b26352
A tall tower and a closed door. No palace at all.
/STR 01413963
Whom are you waiting for? Dulcinea won't come by herself.
/STR 523a317e
You kneel. Nobody knows why. Neither do you.
/STR 4636e97e
A knight doesn't speak to his lady like that, standing up. First he goes down on his knees.
/STR 06923c4d
A round-faced, snub-nosed country girl on a young ass. Sancho swears she is Dulcinea. She smells of raw garlic.
/STR 1e3f1f60
Your own lady! Not even enchanted.
/STR 7bc202ec
Sancho told you to wait for him here, in the oak grove.
/STR 2dbd5dcb
You can't leave without paying your respects to your lady.
/STR 905879b0
You won't leave El Toboso without seeing Dulcinea.
/STR 6efc3492
Death, an angel, an emperor, Cupid and a demon for a driver. All very well dressed for real people.
/STR 51e273d6
Death, with a human face. He looks at you without much interest.
/STR 6939958c
A truly ugly demon, horns and all. He drives the mules.
/STR a8ff080d
Ask who they are first. A knight doesn't charge blindly. Well, he does, but not today.
/STR e6a506ea
The players climb onto the cart. They have a show this afternoon.
/STR 0bc085ee
The cart blocks your way. And those characters are crying out for an explanation.
/STR 22ec087e
With what? Take the lance, the Knight of the Mirrors won't wait.
/STR ecacbae4
A knight doesn't attack another without first knowing who he is. Greet him.
/STR e67a7f98
He hasn't challenged you yet. Answer his question.
/STR e790cc09
You can't leave without knowing who that sighing knight is.
/STR 8787617c
A surcoat sown with mirror moons and a helmet with green, yellow and white plumes.
/STR d6ca017b
I don't think this is the right moment.
/STR f41150f9
To fight a lion, you first have to get him out of his cage.
/STR c5f0077d
You turn your back on the lion. Bad idea.
/STR 1c7cb020
Rocinante won't let you: he has a lion in front of him and more sense than you.
/STR dba03608
The lion looks at you. You look at the lion. The lion licks his lips.
/STR 7589dc84
In the cage, a lion of extraordinary size, of fearful and hideous aspect.
/STR d98d425d
A huge lion, out of his cage, with a hungry look on his face.
/STR 7c30ec3a
The lions' cart blocks your way, and you are not a man to pass by a pair of lions.
/STR 33fe8695
Sancho has already eaten for both of you. For three, in fact.
/STR 2112853a
Six pots as big as wine jars, each able to hold a whole butcher's shop of meat. Sancho looks at them lovingly.
/STR 419a45e1
The cooks have no time for chatter. The wedding is to the north, under the bower.
/STR 77f5e024
A dying man? A bridegroom? Better say something to CAMACHO.
/STR dfb22bb1
Basilio and Quiteria have eyes only for each other. Camacho, only for his pots.
/STR 8a7ecaba
A poor shepherd, nimble and discreet. For a dead man, he has a good colour.
/STR 91630f58
Radiant, with her corals and her thirty-pile velvet. Sancho says it isn't thirty, it's forty.
/STR 6d11e90b
Camacho the rich. Rich, and not much more.
/STR a1df1274
The wedding isn't over and, besides, you don't know where to go.
/STR 44e0c192
Brambles aren't cut by hand. A sword would come in handy.
/STR 688be13b
Boxthorn, wild fig, brambles and briars. They need CUTTING.
/STR 334a7d76
You already have the rope tied round your waist.
/STR ecda3a36
You have no rope.
/STR 6b288097
First you'll have to clear a way through the brambles.
/STR 446c343e
On horseback, down a cave? Dismount.
/STR 8135842f
You've seen enough down there. More than anybody will believe.
/STR 33dd3d4a
The mouth is blinded by brambles and briars.
/STR e1a2f7bc
Without a rope? Not even Montesinos goes down like that.
/STR a54b9b22
You don't enter a cave like this one: you go DOWN.
/STR 87226b70
Nobody sleeps well hanging from a rope. Look for some hollow to lie down in.
/STR 00179454
There's no room to lie down here. Look for some hollow.
/STR e1990f62
"Dulcinea!" you shout. "...ea, ...ea," answers the cave.
/STR 52d529a5
"Patience, and shuffle the cards," answers Durandarte from his tomb. It's all he ever says.
/STR fd47feb5
"Half a dozen reals, sir, on this petticoat," insists Dulcinea's companion.
/STR 44eb4d5b
